1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:14,773
Welcome. This video teaches you Mesiri Accounts: what it does, the accounting behind it, and how to sell it.

2
00:00:15,123 --> 00:00:26,302
By the end, you will be able to sit with a builder and his accountant and answer every question with confidence.

3
00:00:26,652 --> 00:00:33,223
We will follow Anjali, the accountant of a construction company, through her day in Mesiri.

4
00:00:36,573 --> 00:00:44,253
Today, a builder's site team records expenses on WhatsApp, in notebooks and in Excel.

5
00:00:44,603 --> 00:00:51,685
Then an accountant types the same bills again into Tally, weeks later.

6
00:00:52,035 --> 00:01:01,251
So the owner sees his profit only when the CA sends the quarter's figures. By then it is too late to act.

7
00:01:01,601 --> 00:01:14,572
Mesiri Accounts removes that retyping. Every bill, payment and client receipt already in Mesiri becomes an accounting entry on its own.

8
00:01:14,922 --> 00:01:22,858
The owner sees cash, profit and what is owed today, not next quarter. That is our promise.

9
00:01:26,208 --> 00:01:33,888
First, a little accounting. Every rupee has two sides: where it came from, and where it went.

10
00:01:34,238 --> 00:01:41,833
This is called double entry. One side is the debit, the other is the credit. They are always equal.

11
00:01:42,183 --> 00:01:53,020
Example: a cement bill of ₹11,800 from Malabar Steel, with ₹1,800 GST inside it.

12
00:01:53,370 --> 00:02:09,839
Materials gets a debit of ₹10,000. Input GST gets a debit of ₹1,800. Malabar gets a credit of ₹11,800, because we owe them.

13
00:02:10,189 --> 00:02:23,331
Debits ₹11,800, credits ₹11,800. Equal. Mesiri checks this on every single entry, and refuses one that does not balance.

14
00:02:26,681 --> 00:02:31,033
An easy way to remember debit and credit.

15
00:02:31,383 --> 00:02:41,025
Debit is what the company has, or what it spent: cash in the bank, money clients owe us, cement, wages.

16
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:56,650
Credit is where it came from, or whom we owe: suppliers we must pay, the owner's capital, the income from bills to clients.

17
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:12,701
So a supplier's ledger normally shows a credit balance: that is what we owe him. A client's ledger shows a debit: that is what he owes us.

18
00:03:16,051 --> 00:03:27,657
Each account is called a ledger. Every supplier, every client, every bank account and every expense head has its own ledger.

19
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:38,332
Ledgers sit in groups. Mesiri uses the same groups as Tally, so the CA feels at home.

20
00:03:38,682 --> 00:03:51,226
Five kinds: assets, what we own. Liabilities, what we owe. Capital, the owner's money. Income. And expenses.

21
00:03:51,576 --> 00:03:59,085
Remember these five colours. You will see them on every screen of Mesiri Accounts.

22
00:04:02,435 --> 00:04:09,433
Now, the software. Anjali logs in as the accountant. Her role is Finance.

23
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:26,081
Her home screen answers the owner's four questions at once: how much cash do we have, who owes us, whom do we owe, and are we making profit.

24
00:04:26,431 --> 00:04:33,343
Profit this month is compared with last month, with an arrow up or down.

25
00:04:33,693 --> 00:04:48,371
The chart shows the whole financial year, April to March, month by month. Green bars are profit, red bars are loss. Tap a month for its figures.

26
00:04:48,721 --> 00:04:56,145
On the right: each bank and cash account, the GST to pay this month, and the TDS to deposit.

27
00:04:56,495 --> 00:05:07,247
And at the top, a list for the accountant: things that need her attention, like money received with no client set.

28
00:05:10,597 --> 00:05:18,533
Here is the magic, and your strongest selling point. Anjali did not type any of this.

29
00:05:18,883 --> 00:05:26,477
A site engineer sends a bill photo on WhatsApp. Mesiri reads it and records the expense.

30
00:05:26,827 --> 00:05:38,177
The moment it is confirmed, Accounts posts a purchase entry: materials and GST debited, the supplier credited.

31
00:05:38,527 --> 00:05:51,753
When finance pays the supplier, a payment entry. When the client pays an RA bill, a receipt entry. Transfers to site petty cash, a contra entry.

32
00:05:52,103 --> 00:06:02,770
Subcontractor bills with TDS and retention, client RA bills with GST, retention and TDS, all of them, automatically.

33
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:09,349
If a bill is corrected later, the entry corrects itself. Nobody types anything twice.

34
00:06:12,699 --> 00:06:21,489
The day book is every entry, day by day. Accountants call each entry a voucher.

35
00:06:21,839 --> 00:06:33,188
Six kinds of voucher, each with its own colour and icon: purchase, payment, receipt, sales, contra and journal.

36
00:06:33,538 --> 00:06:44,973
Each day shows the money that came in and went out. She can search a supplier, a number, or any word.

37
00:06:45,323 --> 00:06:53,003
She opens a voucher with a tap, or with the arrow keys and Enter, like Tally.

38
00:06:53,353 --> 00:07:02,227
Every voucher shows its debits and credits, the project and site, and a button that opens the original bill with its photo.

39
00:07:02,577 --> 00:07:10,769
This is what makes the CA trust it: every figure traces back to the record it came from.

40
00:07:14,119 --> 00:07:20,093
Every ledger has its statement. Here is Malabar Steel, a supplier.

41
00:07:20,443 --> 00:07:31,963
Each line says what it was against: 'By' for what we owe him, 'To' for what we paid. And a running balance after every line.

42
00:07:32,313 --> 00:07:38,286
The line on top shows how the balance moved over the period.

43
00:07:38,636 --> 00:07:47,511
The closing balance, a credit, is what we still owe Malabar. It matches the supplier page in Finance exactly.

44
00:07:47,861 --> 00:07:58,954
Any ledger can be downloaded to Excel and sent to the supplier or the client. No more arguments over who owes what.

45
00:08:02,304 --> 00:08:10,837
The trial balance lists every ledger's balance. The total debits must equal the total credits.

46
00:08:11,187 --> 00:08:15,283
The green badge at the top says it: balanced.

47
00:08:15,633 --> 00:08:24,081
Groups open to show their ledgers, coloured by their kind, with a bar showing their size.

48
00:08:24,431 --> 00:08:37,146
Mesiri also checks the books against the other modules: every bank account against Finance, every supplier against the supplier ledger, every client against Receivables.

49
00:08:37,496 --> 00:08:46,712
If anything differs, it says so and shows which one. It never hides a difference.

50
00:08:47,062 --> 00:08:58,582
The trial balance works for the whole company, one project or one site, using the selector at the top.

51
00:09:01,932 --> 00:09:09,185
The chart of accounts is the list of all groups and ledgers.

52
00:09:09,535 --> 00:09:22,421
It comes ready, with Tally's groups and the ledgers a builder needs: contract revenue, GST, TDS, retention, mobilisation advance.

53
00:09:22,771 --> 00:09:34,291
Each supplier, client, bank account and expense head gets its ledger on its own, the first time it is used. Nothing to set up.

54
00:09:34,641 --> 00:09:43,771
The accountant can still add ledgers, rename them, or move them to another group, the way her CA likes it.

55
00:09:47,121 --> 00:09:50,705
The profit and loss statement. First, the idea.

56
00:09:51,055 --> 00:10:06,330
Income is what we billed clients, before GST. Direct costs are what the work itself cost: materials, labour, subcontractors, machines.

57
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:18,968
Income minus direct costs is the gross profit. Then subtract the office costs, the indirect expenses, and you get the net profit.

58
00:10:19,318 --> 00:10:25,633
In Mesiri, it reads exactly like that, with the margin as a percentage.

59
00:10:25,983 --> 00:10:38,015
She can compare with the period before, or the same period last year. Arrows show what went up or down.

60
00:10:38,365 --> 00:10:45,191
Tap a group to see its ledgers, and a ledger to see its entries.

61
00:10:48,541 --> 00:10:58,099
The balance sheet is a photograph of one day: what the company owns, against what it owes plus the owner's capital.

62
00:10:58,449 --> 00:11:03,910
Both sides must be equal. That is why it is called a balance sheet.

63
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:12,025
Mesiri shows the two sides next to each other, for any date, with this year's profit added to capital.

64
00:11:15,375 --> 00:11:24,079
Cash and bank shows every bank account and site petty cash box: opening, money in, money out, closing.

65
00:11:24,429 --> 00:11:35,779
The chart shows each day's money in and out. Moves between our own accounts are left out, so it shows real money only.

66
00:11:36,129 --> 00:11:41,334
The balances are the same as the Money page in Finance, to the rupee.

67
00:11:44,684 --> 00:11:48,951
This is the screen owners love: which project makes money.

68
00:11:49,301 --> 00:11:57,749
For every project: billed, cost, profit and margin, and what the client owes and what we owe for it.

69
00:11:58,099 --> 00:12:08,509
Open a project and you see its position: billed, received, still owed, retention the client is holding, TDS the client deducted.

70
00:12:08,859 --> 00:12:18,075
Where the cost went, by cost centre: materials, labour, subcontract, machines.

71
00:12:18,425 --> 00:12:28,239
And the BOQ budget from Cost Control right beside the books. If the forecast goes over the budget, it says so in red.

72
00:12:28,589 --> 00:12:36,695
A project manager can see his own project's books, but never the company's.

73
00:12:37,045 --> 00:12:50,699
If the owner wants, office costs like rent and salaries can be shared over the projects each month, so each project's profit is after its share.

74
00:12:54,049 --> 00:13:00,193
Now GST. Every builder worries about it, so learn this well.

75
00:13:00,543 --> 00:13:10,356
Output GST is the GST we charge clients on RA bills. Input GST is the GST we paid on purchases.

76
00:13:10,706 --> 00:13:19,837
We pay the government output minus input. Input GST we can deduct is called input tax credit.

77
00:13:20,187 --> 00:13:32,731
But input credit is allowed only if the supplier has a GSTIN on the bill. A bill from a supplier with no GSTIN: that GST is lost.

78
00:13:33,081 --> 00:13:45,027
Within Kerala, GST is split half CGST and half SGST. With a party in another state, it is IGST.

79
00:13:48,377 --> 00:13:56,313
Mesiri's GST page gives the month's figures in the return's own terms, ready for the CA.

80
00:13:56,663 --> 00:14:07,757
Output GST, input GST we can claim, and the net to pay, split into IGST, CGST and SGST.

81
00:14:08,107 --> 00:14:19,797
And the credit missed: bills whose GST cannot be claimed, and why. Usually, the supplier's GSTIN is missing.

82
00:14:20,147 --> 00:14:33,971
Tell the customer this: Mesiri shows the money you are losing on GST every month, while there is still time to fix it. That alone can pay for Mesiri.

83
00:14:34,321 --> 00:14:43,708
The green badge says the GST figures agree with the books. And everything downloads to Excel for the CA.

84
00:14:47,058 --> 00:15:03,186
TDS means tax deducted at source. When we pay a subcontractor, the law says we keep back a small part, one or two percent, under section 194C.

85
00:15:03,536 --> 00:15:10,875
That money belongs to the government. It must be deposited by the 7th of the next month. Late means interest.

86
00:15:11,225 --> 00:15:24,025
Clients do the same to us: they deduct TDS from our RA bills. That TDS is ours to claim against our tax.

87
00:15:27,375 --> 00:15:37,017
Mesiri deducts TDS when a subcontractor's bill is checked, and keeps it in a TDS payable account.

88
00:15:37,367 --> 00:15:46,839
The TDS page shows each month: deducted, deposited, still to deposit, and the due date. Late months turn red.

89
00:15:47,189 --> 00:15:58,624
It lists each subcontractor with his PAN, and the TDS clients deducted from our bills, to match with Form 26AS.

90
00:16:01,974 --> 00:16:09,910
Once the CA has checked a month, Anjali closes it. Closed months are locked.

91
00:16:10,260 --> 00:16:22,633
If a bill from a closed month is changed later, the site is never blocked. The difference is posted on the first open day, and marked as changed after close.

92
00:16:22,983 --> 00:16:34,674
Only the owner or an admin can reopen a month, and they must give a reason. This is the control accountants and auditors look for.

93
00:16:38,024 --> 00:16:43,400
Know the roles. The owner, admins and finance see the books.

94
00:16:43,750 --> 00:16:50,406
A project manager sees only his own projects' profit and position.

95
00:16:50,756 --> 00:16:58,863
Site engineers and supervisors never see money in the books. They just keep sending their updates on WhatsApp.

96
00:16:59,213 --> 00:17:06,551
Accounts is an add-on module. The Mesiri team switches it on for a company.

97
00:17:09,901 --> 00:17:13,571
Now, how to sell it. Four points.

98
00:17:13,921 --> 00:17:24,246
One: no double typing. Site records become the books on their own. The accountant reviews instead of retyping.

99
00:17:24,596 --> 00:17:29,289
Two: profit today, per project, not after the quarter.

100
00:17:29,639 --> 00:17:36,551
Three: GST credit that would have been lost is shown every month.

101
00:17:36,901 --> 00:17:49,957
Four: it speaks the CA's language, Tally's groups, Tally export and Excel, so the CA is your friend, not your enemy.

102
00:17:53,307 --> 00:17:55,867
Questions you will hear.

103
00:17:56,217 --> 00:18:10,297
'We already use Tally.' Answer: keep it. Mesiri keeps the books from the site, and exports vouchers to Tally for the CA. No retyping.

104
00:18:10,647 --> 00:18:26,605
'Can I trust the numbers?' Answer: every entry must balance, or it is refused. And the books are checked against bank, suppliers and clients every time.

105
00:18:26,955 --> 00:18:39,072
'Will my accountant lose her job?' Answer: no. She stops typing and starts checking. Her work becomes more valuable.

106
00:18:39,422 --> 00:18:48,297
'What about wages?' Answer: wages enter the books when they are paid. Attendance stays in the Labour module.

107
00:18:51,647 --> 00:18:58,815
Let's recap. Every rupee has two sides, debit and credit, always equal.

108
00:18:59,165 --> 00:19:12,989
Mesiri turns site records into the books automatically: day book, ledgers, trial balance, profit and loss, balance sheet, cash and bank.

109
00:19:13,339 --> 00:19:22,981
Project profit, GST and TDS are ready every month, and the CA gets Excel and Tally.

110
00:19:23,331 --> 00:19:30,670
You now know more accounting than most of our customers. Go and sell it with confidence. Thank you.

